WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.784 --> 00:00:19.615
In the 1866, something remarkable happened at sea, a mysterious and baffling phenomenon.

2
00:00:20.476 --> 00:00:26.800
Although sailors were especially excited about it, the general public—even those who lived far inland—heard the rumors too.

3
00:00:27.761 --> 00:00:31.944
For some time vessels had been encountering "an enormous thing" at sea.

4
00:00:32.864 --> 00:00:35.345
This thing was a long, spindle-shaped object.

5
00:00:36.026 --> 00:00:41.148
People said it occasionally glowed, and it was larger and more rapid in its movements than any whale.

6
00:00:42.069 --> 00:00:48.032
On July 20 the steamship Governor Higginson met the thing five miles off the east coast of Australia.

7
00:00:48.913 --> 00:00:52.815
At first Captain Baker thought he was in the presence of an unknown sandbank.

8
00:00:53.695 --> 00:00:58.858
He was about to determine its exact position in the ocean when two columns of water shot a

9
00:00:59.038 --> 00:01:02.580
hundred and fifty feet into the air with a loud hissing noise.

10
00:01:03.616 --> 00:01:09.820
Three days later another ship spotted the thing—but this ship was fifteen hundred miles away in the Pacific!

11
00:01:11.281 --> 00:01:17.104
Fifteen days later and two thousand miles away, two more ships encountered the mysterious object.

12
00:01:18.065 --> 00:01:19.786
Everyone began talking about the thing.

13
00:01:20.486 --> 00:01:26.010
They sang about it in the cafes, ridiculed it in the newspapers, and showcased it on the stage.

14
00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:33.545
All kinds of stories circulated about the object, causing editors of scientific journals to quarrel with believers in

15
00:01:33.565 --> 00:01:34.365
the supernatural.

16
00:01:35.306 --> 00:01:40.987
The subject was debated for six months until a journal poked such fun at the topic that stories

17
00:01:41.047 --> 00:01:42.848
about the strange thing finally died.

18
00:01:43.708 --> 00:01:45.108
The topic seemed buried forever.

19
00:01:45.769 --> 00:01:49.970
But during the first months of 1867, new facts came to light.

20
00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:56.692
Suddenly the thing at sea was no longer a scientific problem to be solved but a real danger

21
00:01:56.852 --> 00:01:57.512
to be avoided.

22
00:01:58.448 --> 00:02:05.495
On March 5, 1867, a Canadian passenger ship, the Moravian, struck a rock that wasn't marked on any

23
00:02:05.595 --> 00:02:07.456
map for that part of the Atlantic Ocean.

24
00:02:08.938 --> 00:02:15.063
Had it not been for the ship's superior strength, the Moravian would have gone down with all 237

25
00:02:15.804 --> 00:02:16.865
passengers onboard.

26
00:02:17.806 --> 00:02:19.828
The accident happened just as day was breaking.

27
00:02:20.528 --> 00:02:24.012
The officers rushed to the rear of the ship and carefully studied the sea.

28
00:02:25.004 --> 00:02:30.445
But they saw nothing except the surface of the water violently swirling about 1800 feet away.

29
00:02:31.386 --> 00:02:37.247
The exact location of the place was noted, and the Moravian continued on its route without apparent damage.

30
00:02:38.188 --> 00:02:40.848
Had it struck a rock or an enormous shipwreck?

31
00:02:41.608 --> 00:02:43.229
The ship's officers had no idea.

32
00:02:44.089 --> 00:02:49.050
But when the ship's bottom was examined while undergoing repairs, workers found that part of the keel was

33
00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:54.032
broken. This incident might have been forgotten if the same thing hadn't happened a little more than a

34
00:02:54.072 --> 00:02:54.572
month later.

35
00:02:55.568 --> 00:03:00.630
Only in this case, the event caused a huge uproar because of the excellent reputation of the shipping

36
00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:03.592
line involved—the famous Cunard line of England.

37
00:03:05.092 --> 00:03:12.475
In 26 years Cunard ships had successfully made two thousand Atlantic crossings without a voyage canceled, a delay

38
00:03:12.515 --> 00:03:16.857
recorded, or a man, a ship, or even a letter lost.

39
00:03:17.878 --> 00:03:25.485
On April 13, 1867, with a smooth sea and a moderate breeze, the Cunard's Scotia was crossing the

40
00:03:25.525 --> 00:03:27.046
Atlantic, headed for England.

41
00:03:27.986 --> 00:03:33.088
At a little after four o'clock in the afternoon, while the passengers were having tea, a slight shock

42
00:03:33.148 --> 00:03:33.589
was felt.

43
00:03:34.469 --> 00:03:38.751
The Scotia had not struck anything; instead it had been struck.

44
00:03:39.711 --> 00:03:42.573
The shock had been so slight that no one was alarmed at first.

45
00:03:43.273 --> 00:03:46.454
But then a member of the crew started shouting, "We're sinking!

46
00:03:46.815 --> 00:03:47.415
We're sinking!"

47
00:03:48.275 --> 00:03:53.871
The passengers were very afraid, so Captain Anderson quickly reassured them that the ship was not in danger

48
00:03:53.911 --> 00:03:54.392
of sinking.

49
00:03:55.292 --> 00:04:00.837
The Scotia, divided into seven compartments by strong partitions, could withstand any leak.

50
00:04:02.318 --> 00:04:06.502
Captain Anderson soon found that the sea was rapidly pouring into the fifth compartment.

51
00:04:07.382 --> 00:04:11.506
He ordered the engines to be stopped at once, and one of the men dove into the water

52
00:04:11.606 --> 00:04:12.687
to determine the damage.

53
00:04:13.487 --> 00:04:16.310
He found a large hole, two yards in diameter.

54
00:04:17.271 --> 00:04:21.537
Such a leak could not be stopped, so the Scotia was forced to continue on its way.

55
00:04:22.418 --> 00:04:27.560
The ship was then about three hundred miles from Ireland, and after three days' delay, it finally reached

56
00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:28.280
a British port.

57
00:04:29.161 --> 00:04:33.083
When the Scotia was examined more carefully, the hole was found to be in the shape of a

58
00:04:33.163 --> 00:04:39.806
triangle. Something had punched its way through the ship's thick iron plates and then pulled out.

59
00:04:40.686 --> 00:04:44.328
From this moment on, the monster was blamed for any accident at sea.

60
00:04:45.228 --> 00:04:50.770
During the time that these developments were taking place, I was returning from a scientific expedition to explore

61
00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:52.991
the Nebraska Territory in the United States.

62
00:04:54.492 --> 00:05:01.175
The French government had sent me, Pierre Aronnax, on the expedition because I am an assistant professor at

63
00:05:01.195 --> 00:05:03.255
the Museum of Natural History in Paris.

64
00:05:04.196 --> 00:05:09.218
After six months in Nebraska, I had arrived in New York City with a precious collection of minerals,

65
00:05:09.698 --> 00:05:11.079
plants, and animals.

66
00:05:12.079 --> 00:05:16.573
I was not due to return to France for a while, so I was busy organizing my specimens

67
00:05:16.753 --> 00:05:18.294
when the Scotia incident occurred.

68
00:05:19.254 --> 00:05:22.896
I was well aware of this unfortunate event and the debate over its cause.

69
00:05:23.976 --> 00:05:24.816
How could I not have been?

70
00:05:25.637 --> 00:05:31.199
I had read and reread every American and European newspaper without being able to form a conclusion.

71
00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:34.441
Something was out there; that much was certain.

72
00:05:35.361 --> 00:05:37.982
While I was in New York, the issue reached a boiling point.

73
00:05:38.962 --> 00:05:44.606
People had discussed a theory about a drifting island or reef, but that idea had been completely eliminated.

74
00:05:46.067 --> 00:05:51.492
How could an island or reef move at such fantastic speed unless it had an engine in its

75
00:05:51.532 --> 00:05:57.357
belly? For the same reason, the idea of a massive, floating shipwreck had also been abandoned.

76
00:05:58.258 --> 00:06:04.083
There were only two possible explanations for the thing, and each explanation had its supporters.

77
00:06:05.224 --> 00:06:09.288
On one side were those who were sure it was a monster of colossal strength.

78
00:06:10.312 --> 00:06:15.694
On the other side were those who believed the thing was some type of underwater boat with tremendous

79
00:06:15.794 --> 00:06:16.374
engine power.

80
00:06:17.314 --> 00:06:19.755
But this second idea seemed nearly impossible.

81
00:06:20.515 --> 00:06:23.896
How could a private individual have a vessel like this at his command?

82
00:06:24.856 --> 00:06:28.457
Where had it been built, and how had its construction been kept secret?

83
00:06:29.438 --> 00:06:33.919
It was possible, however, that a nation might construct a submarine to use as a weapon of war.

84
00:06:35.379 --> 00:06:37.780
But rival countries are always watching each other.

85
00:06:38.516 --> 00:06:43.340
It was unlikely that one nation could have built a submarine without another nation knowing about it.

86
00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:51.365
Inquiries were made in England, France, Russia, Prussia, Spain, Italy, America, and even in Turkey.

87
00:06:52.346 --> 00:06:57.109
When the inquiries produced no facts, the hypothesis of a submarine was rejected.

88
00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:01.212
And so the idea of a monster surfaced again.

89
00:07:02.053 --> 00:07:06.396
Upon my arrival in New York, several people did me the honor of consulting me on the subject.

90
00:07:07.348 --> 00:07:12.510
In France I had published a two-volume work, Mysteries of the Great Underwater World.

91
00:07:13.471 --> 00:07:19.894
Well-regarded in academic circles, my book gave me a reputation as an expert in this obscure branch of

92
00:07:19.974 --> 00:07:20.714
natural history.

93
00:07:21.634 --> 00:07:25.456
By the time the New York Herald asked me to write an article on the subject, I had

94
00:07:25.496 --> 00:07:27.137
my own ideas about this monster.
